<div class="article-body">

<p>I killed my first batch in 2 weeks. The leaves turned yellow, the stems went limp, and within days everything was wilting. I had no idea what went wrong. I thought hydroponics was supposed to be foolproof. The internet said it was easy. My plants disagreed.</p>

<p>I cleaned everything and tried again. Batch 2 lasted 3 weeks before the same symptoms appeared. This time the water smelled bad and my pump was barely moving water. I was frustrated. I almost gave up.</p>

<p>Batch 3 was the worst. I spent $85 on new nutrients and a better pump. The plants grew great for 10 days. Then the roots clogged the pump intake and the whole system stopped. I lost $40 worth of seedlings in one afternoon. That's when I sat down and figured out the root causes for real. I had lost roughly $200 across all three failures. Seeds, nutrients, replacement parts, wasted electricity.</p>

<p>Here's the thing I learned: hydroponic problems are almost always caused by one of five things. And none of them are hard to fix once you know what to look for. But nobody tells you this upfront. The guides I found were either too basic or assumed you already knew the terminology. So here's the version I wish I had found.</p>

<p>Full disclosure: I'm not a scientist. I'm just someone who killed a lot of plants before figuring out what works. If I can get this right, you can too.</p>

<h2 id="ph">1. pH Imbalance: The Silent Killer</h2>

<p>Your plants can't absorb nutrients if the pH is wrong. Period. I learned this after batch 1 when I checked my pH for the first time. It was 8.2. My lettuce needed 5.5 to 6.5. I had been feeding them the right nutrients, but they couldn't actually use them. Imagine being in a room full of food but your mouth is sewn shut. That's what pH 8.2 does to a plant.</p>

<p>The fix is simple: get a decent pH meter. Not the $8 ones on Amazon. Those drift after a week and give you bad readings. I use the <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Apera+PH60+pH+meter&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Apera PH60</a>. It was $48 at last check. It's the only piece of equipment I didn't cheap out on, and it's still accurate 6 months later. Test every 2 days. Keep it between 5.5 and 6.5. Use pH Up or pH Down to adjust.</p>

<p>Here's the trick most guides won't tell you: pH drifts naturally as plants consume nutrients. If your pH keeps climbing, your plants are drinking faster than they're eating. If it keeps dropping, you're overfeeding. The pH reading tells you what's happening before your leaves do. I check mine in 30 seconds while making morning coffee. Quick habit, massive payoff.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-title">Quick Test</div>
  <p>If your plants look sick and you haven't checked pH in a week, that's your first stop. 80% of my early problems were pH related. Fixing it fixed everything else.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="root-rot">2. Root Rot: The Smell Test</h2>

<p>Healthy roots are white or light beige. Unhealthy roots are brown, slimy, and smell like a swamp. I learned this from batch 2. When I pulled out my plants, the roots were dark brown and the water had a rotten egg smell. The plants looked fine on top until day 21, then collapsed in 48 hours.</p>

<p>Root rot happens when oxygen levels drop. In a DWC system, that means your air stone is too small or your pump is failing. In a Kratky system, it means the water level is too high and the roots are drowning. The roots need oxygen just like the leaves need light. Cut off either one and they die.</p>

<p>Fix it fast: trim the brown roots, rinse the healthy ones in cool water, and add 3% hydrogen peroxide at 2ml per liter. Then fix the underlying problem. For DWC, upgrade your air pump. For Kratky, lower the water level so the roots get air space. After I fixed my oxygenation, my next batch had white roots for the full 8 weeks. Night and day difference.</p>

<p>If you're not sure whether you have root rot, check the smell first. If your reservoir smells earthy and fresh, you're fine. If it smells like decay or eggs, you have a problem. Trust your nose. It will tell you before your eyes will.</p>

<h2 id="pump">3. Pump Failure: Your System's Heart</h2>

<p>My batch 3 failure was a pump clog. Roots grew into the pump intake and stopped it dead. I didn't notice for 48 hours because the water was still moving. Slowly, but moving. By the time I realized something was wrong, the water was stagnant and the roots were suffocating.</p>

<p>Check your pump weekly. If the flow is weaker than usual, the impeller might be clogged. Unplug it, take it apart, clean it with a soft brush. Most pumps can be rebuilt in under 10 minutes. I clean mine every Sunday while checking pH. It adds 5 minutes to my routine and saves me from losing another batch.</p>

<p>The hidden problem nobody mentions: cheap pumps fail differently than good ones. A $15 pump just stops. A <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Vivosun+air+pump+4+outlet&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">$30 Vivosun pump</a> loses flow gradually giving you time to notice and fix it. I switched after batch 3 and haven't had a clog since. The extra $15 was worth saving an entire crop. Add <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=air+pump+check+valve+hydroponics&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">check valves</a> to prevent backflow if power cuts out. Another $5 for peace of mind.</p>

<h2 id="algae">4. Algae: More Than Just Ugly</h2>

<p>Algae turns your reservoir green and competes with your plants for nutrients. It also swings pH more than almost anything else I've seen. I ignored green water for 3 days once. By day 4, my pH had jumped from 6.0 to 7.8 and my plants were showing nitrogen deficiency.</p>

<p>Algae needs three things: light, water, and nutrients. Block any one and it dies. The easiest fix is blocking light from reaching your reservoir. Use opaque containers. Cover any exposed water surface with foil or light-blocking tape. I use 5-gallon buckets from Home Depot ($4 each) painted black. I sealed every hole with electrical tape. No light gets in.</p>

<p>The difference was immediate. Before the fix, I was changing nutrients every 5 days because the algae consumed everything. After blocking light, I changed every 2 weeks. Same plants, same nutrients, half the work. If you have clear tubing in your system, wrap it with foil too. Algae grows inside clear tubing faster than you'd think.</p>

<h2 id="nutrients">5. Nutrient Burn: Too Much of a Good Thing</h2>

<p>More nutrients does not mean more growth. I overdosed my plants in batch 1 because I thought if a little is good, more must be better. Wrong. The leaf tips turned brown and curled up. Classic nutrient burn. The leaves looked like they had been singed with a lighter.</p>

<p>Follow the feeding chart on your nutrient bottle. Start at half strength for seedlings. I use <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=General+Hydroponics+Flora+Series&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">General Hydroponics Flora Series</a>. A $35 kit lasts me 4 months across 6 plants. For beginners, it's the most forgiving brand I've tried. Mix at half strength for the first 2 weeks, then full strength after that. If you see brown tips, cut back immediately and flush with plain pH-adjusted water for 24 hours.</p>

<p>Here's another thing I learned the hard way: different plants need different amounts. Basil eats more than lettuce. Lettuce eats more than microgreens. The same nutrient mix that makes your tomatoes explode will burn your lettuce to a crisp. Don't treat all plants the same.</p>

<p>If you're troubleshooting leaf discoloration, check out <a href="/posts/hydroponic-leaves-turning-yellow">my guide on yellow leaves in hydroponics</a> for a full symptom-to-cause flowchart. It covers the exact differences between nitrogen deficiency and pH lockout.</p>

<h2 id="tools">6. The Tools That Actually Fix These Problems</h2>

<p>So now you know the five things that can kill your hydroponic system. But knowing what can go wrong is one thing. Actually fixing it without buying the wrong tools is another. Let me show you exactly what I use, what I tested, and what I wasted money on along the way.</p>

<p>The cheap pH test strips ($8 for 100 strips) work for quick checks. But they're hard to read and you might second-guess yourself. A bad reading means bad adjustments. I used these for my first month and I'm pretty sure half my pH readings were wrong.</p>

<p>At the other extreme, $150 lab-grade pH meters are overkill for a home setup. You're growing lettuce, not running a research lab.</p>

<p>The sweet spot is the <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Apera+PH60+pH+meter&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Apera PH60</a>. It's $48, auto-calibrates, and stays accurate for months without re-calibration. One honest thing about it: the storage cap needs a few drops of solution to keep the probe wet. I forgot once and the reading drifted by 0.4. It calibrated back after soaking for an hour. Minor inconvenience but worth noting.</p>

<p>For air pumps: the $12 single-outlet pumps from Amazon are fine for one bucket. But they're loud and fail fast. I killed two in 3 months. The <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Vivosun+air+pump+4+outlet&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Vivosun 4-outlet pump</a> runs quieter and has replaceable parts. I've had mine for 6 months with zero issues. Add <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=air+pump+check+valve+hydroponics&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">check valves</a> to prevent backflow during power outages. Another $5 well spent.</p>

<p>For nutrients: the <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=General+Hydroponics+Flora+Series&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">General Hydroponics Flora Series</a> 3-part kit. I started with a $15 no-name kit and got inconsistent growth. The GH kit is the standard for a reason. It's stable, it works, and there's enough documentation online to fix any issue. It's the Toyota Camry of hydroponic nutrients.</p>

<p>If light distance or intensity is part of your problem, check out <a href="https://growlightdb.tabletopharvest.com" target="_blank">Grow Light DB</a> for detailed comparisons. Bad light setup can cause symptoms that look exactly like nutrient deficiencies.</p>

<p><strong>Bottom line:</strong> If your system is struggling, start with pH. That's what killed 2 of my 3 batches. Test it. Adjust it. Then move down the list: roots, pump, algae, nutrients. Nine times out of ten, the fix is something simple that you overlooked. I spent $200 learning this lesson so you don't have to. <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Apera+PH60+pH+meter&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Check current price on Amazon</a>. It was $48 when I last checked. For what it saves you in dead plants, it pays for itself on the first batch.</p>

